---
layout: base.njk
permalink: /index.html
---

{% from "statbar.njk" import statbar %}
{% from "stacks.njk" import technology %}

{#
	The page's own sections, built from the same conditions the sections use so
	an entry never points at something that was not rendered. Categories are a
	separate list further down: those go to other pages, and a table of contents
	that leaves the page is not one.

	Sticky, like the site page's — this page is long, and the sections below the
	leaderboard are a thousand rows out of sight.
#}
{% set toc = [] %}
{% if sites.groups.length > 1 or sites.moving.length or sites.orphans.length %}
	{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "categories", label: "Categories" }), toc) %}
{% endif %}
{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "summary", label: "Summary" }), toc) %}
{% if sites.stats.measured %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "leaderboard", label: "Perfect scores" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if sites.stacks.generators.items.length or sites.stacks.hosts.items.length %}
	{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "stacks", label: "Technology" }), toc) %}
{% endif %}
{% if sites.moving.length %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "moving", label: "Redirects" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if sites.orphans.length %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "orphans", label: "Orphaned" }), toc) %}{% endif %}
{% if sites.archived.length %}{% set toc = (toc.push({ id: "archived", label: "Archived" }), toc) %}{% endif %}

{% if sites.groups.length > 1 or sites.moving.length or sites.orphans.length %}
<section class="group" id="categories">
	<div class="group-head">
		<h2 id="categories-heading">Categories</h2>
	</div>
	{# Labeled by the section's heading rather than an aria-label, so the name a
	   screen reader announces is the one on the page. #}
	<nav class="contents contents-categories" aria-labelledby="categories-heading">
	<ul>
		{#
			`showOnHomePageCategoryList` is about this list and nothing else, and it
			is off unless a category asks for it — the front page stays short by
			default. A category left out keeps its page, its place in the switcher,
			and every ranking its sites appear in. It is an editorial decision about
			what belongs on a front page, not a way to retire a category.
		#}
		{% for group in sites.groups %}
		{% if group.entries.length and group.showOnHomePageCategoryList %}
		<li>
			<a href="/group/{{ group.id }}/">{{ group.name }}</a>
			{#
				Sites with data, not sites listed. Measurement is rolling — a category
				is added in one commit and fills in over the following days — so the
				listed total counts URLs somebody typed, while this counts what the
				page behind the link can actually show you.
			#}
			<span class="contents-count" title="{{ group.measured | num }} of {{ group.entries.length | num }} {{ group.entries.length | plural('site') }} in {{ group.name }} {{ group.measured | verb('has', 'have') }} data">×{{ group.measured | num }}</span>
			{#
				How long ago anything in this category was last measured. Measurement
				is rolling and each category sets its own cadence, so the curated
				handfuls refresh daily while the big imported lists take days to come
				round — a figure worth showing rather than leaving to be inferred.
			#}
			{% if group.lastMeasured %}
			<span class="contents-age" title="Most recently measured site in {{ group.name }}: {{ group.lastMeasured | date('long') }}"><time datetime="{{ group.lastMeasured | iso }}">{{ group.lastMeasured | since }}</time> ago</span>
			{% endif %}
		</li>
		{% endif %}
		{% endfor %}
		{% if sites.orphans.length %}
		<li>
			<a href="#orphans">Orphaned {{ sites.orphans.length | plural("history", "histories") }}</a>
			<span class="contents-count">{{ sites.orphans.length }}</span>
		</li>
		{% endif %}
	</ul>
</nav>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if toc.length > 1 %}
<nav class="contents contents-sticky" aria-label="On this page">
	<span class="contents-label">On this page</span>
	<ul>
		{% for item in toc %}
		<li><a href="#{{ item.id }}">{{ item.label }}</a></li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
</nav>
{% endif %}

<section class="group statbar-section" id="summary">
	<div class="group-head">
		<h2>Summary</h2>
	</div>
	{#
		The site of the day is a fact about the whole corpus rather than about one
		category, so it is passed in rather than computed per set — the category
		pages call the same macro without it and simply do not show the card.
	#}
	{{ statbar(sites.stats, metrics, sites.siteOfTheDay, meta.screenshotService) }}
</section>

{% if not sites.stats.measured %}
<div class="empty">
	<h2>No measurements yet</h2>
	<p>Run a measurement pass to populate this page:</p>
	<pre><code>npm run measure</code></pre>
	<p class="muted">
		Sites are configured in <code>config/sites.js</code>. For real-user Core Web
		Vitals — including INP, which Lighthouse cannot measure in a lab run — set
		<code>CRUX_API_KEY</code> and run <code>npx speedlify backfill</code> to seed
		six months of field history.
	</p>
</div>
{% endif %}


{#
	One ranked list across every category. The category pages carry the same
	table filtered and re-ranked within themselves; this is the overall view, so
	`ranks.overall` rather than `groupRanks`.
#}
{% set perfectTotal = metrics.SCORES.length * 100 %}
{#
	Both counts are computed before the section so the heading can state them.
	`shown` is every perfect site — not a truncation.

	`entry.perfect` rather than the rule spelled out here: it means full marks in
	every Lighthouse category and nothing amber or red beside them, and it is
	decided in lib/report.js so this table and the count in the stat tile above
	cannot drift apart. The exclusion beside it is the one thing the tile also
	applies — categories that opt out with `excludeFromPerfectScores`, currently
	the starters, whose near-empty demo pages would crowd out real sites.

	The Eleventy share of these is counted in the report instead — the stat tile
	that shows it renders above this, and `{% set %}` inside a loop does not
	survive the loop in Nunjucks.
#}
{% set shown = [] %}
{% for entry in sites.entries %}
	{% if entry.perfect and not entry.excludedFromPerfectScores %}{% set shown = (shown.push(entry), shown) %}{% endif %}
{% endfor %}
<section class="group" id="leaderboard">
	<div class="group-head">
		{#
			The row count beside the heading. `shown` is every qualifying site, not
			a page of them, so this is the size of the board rather than of a view
			onto it — and it answers the first question the table raises without
			making anyone scroll to the end to count.
		#}
		<h2>Perfect scores
			<span class="head-count" title="{{ shown.length | num }} of {{ sites.stats.measured | num }} measured {{ sites.stats.measured | plural('site') }} score {{ perfectTotal }} with nothing amber or red beside it">{{ shown.length | num }} {{ shown.length | plural('site') }}</span>
		</h2>
		<p class="muted">
			These websites score {{ perfectTotal }} in all {{ metrics.SCORES.length }} Lighthouse categories, have no Axe CLI violations, and have passing (or unknown) Core Web Vitals. Tiebreakers on Speed Index per KB, plus TTFB and blocking time — a heavy site outranks an empty site.
		</p>
		{#
			Stated rather than left to be noticed. A starter is a near-empty
			demonstration of a stack, so full marks there is the expected result
			rather than an achievement, and enough of them would crowd out the sites
			this board exists to show. They are still ranked on their own category
			page and in the overall leaderboard below.

			Counted from the entries rather than hardcoded, so the sentence cannot
			outlive the decision — a category dropping `excludeFromPerfectScores`
			leaves nothing here to say.
		#}
		{% set excluded = [] %}
		{% for entry in sites.entries %}
			{% if entry.perfect and entry.excludedFromPerfectScores %}{% set excluded = (excluded.push(entry), excluded) %}{% endif %}
		{% endfor %}
		{% if excluded.length %}
		<p class="muted small">
			Excludes {{ excluded.length }} perfect scoring <a href="/group/11ty-starters/">starter projects</a>. They are shown on their own category page.
		</p>
		{% endif %}
	</div>

	<div class="table-scroll">
	<table class="grid">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-rank">#</th>
				<th class="col-site">Site</th>
				<th class="col-stack" title="What built the site, detected from its generator tag or presumed from the list it is on">Tool</th>
				<th class="col-stack" title="Who serves the site, detected from its response headers on the same page load">Host</th>
				<th class="col-rings" title="The four Lighthouse categories, then violating nodes from a full axe run and the Core Web Vitals verdict from real users. The ranking breaks ties the other way round: Core Web Vitals first, then axe">Scores</th>
				<th class="col-trend">Trend</th>
				<th class="col-lcp"><span class="lcp-head">LCP</span></th>
				<th>CLS</th>
				<th>TBT</th>
				<th>Weight</th>
				<th>Req</th>
				<th class="col-lvf" title="The lab LCP as a multiple of the real-user p75. Above 1x the lab is slower than reality; below 1x the lab number is flattering the site. Only where CrUX has field data">Lab vs field</th>
				<th class="col-nojs" title="Share of pixels that differ between the page as rendered and the same page with scripts disabled. A shifted layout counts, so a noscript banner alone can account for a large figure. A warning mark means the page drew nothing at all without JavaScript. Only measured where a no-JS capture is stored">No-JS ∆</th>
				{#
					Last, and only on this table. The board is drawn from every
					category at once, so "which list is this from?" is a question only
					here — the category pages already answer it in their heading.
				#}
				<th class="col-cats">Categories</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
		{#
			Not a truncation any more: this is every perfect site. The ceiling is
			derived from the score list rather than hardcoded to 400, so adding or
			removing a Lighthouse category cannot silently empty the table.
			`sites.entries` arrives already ranked, so filtering preserves that order.
		#}
		{% for entry in shown %}
			<tr{% if entry.currentlyFailing %} class="row-failing"{% endif %}>
				{#
					Position in this table, not the global rank. The board is a filtered
					view — perfect scores only, starters excluded — so the global rank
					skips every row that was filtered out, and a list of 130 rows ending
					at #158 reads as a bug rather than as a leaderboard.

					`shown` preserves the global ordering, so counting rows says the same
					thing about who beat whom while staying contiguous. The real rank is
					on hover, and the trophies follow it rather than the row number: an
					actual site's medal should not move because a starter above it was
					hidden.
				#}
				<td class="col-rank" title="{% if entry.ranks.overall %}#{{ entry.ranks.overall }} of {{ sites.stats.measured | num }} overall{% else %}unranked{% endif %}">
					<span class="trophy">{{ entry.ranks.overall | trophy }}</span><span class="hash">#</span>{{ loop.index }}{% if entry.ranks.overallClimb %}<span class="rank-climb" title="Up {{ entry.ranks.overallClimb }} {{ "place" if entry.ranks.overallClimb == 1 else "places" }} since the previous measurement, from #{{ entry.ranks.overallPrevious }}">&#9650;{{ entry.ranks.overallClimb }}</span>{% endif %}
				</td>
				<td class="col-site">
					<a class="site-link" href="/site/{{ entry.slug }}/">
						{% set avatar = entry.url | avatar(meta.avatarService) %}
						{% if avatar %}<img class="favicon" src="{{ avatar }}" width="32" height="32" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">{% endif %}
						<span title="{{ entry.displayUrl }}">{{ entry.displayUrl }}</span>
					</a>
					{% if entry.latest %}<span class="updated-tag {{ 'stale-age' if entry.dataStale }}" title="Measured {{ entry.latest.date | date('long') }}"><time datetime="{{ entry.latest.date | iso }}">{{ entry.latest.date | since }}</time></span>{% else %}<span class="updated-tag never">never</span>{% endif %}
					{% if entry.currentlyFailing %}<span class="badge badge-error" title="{{ entry.lastError }}">failing</span>{% endif %}
					{% if entry.redirectTo and entry.redirectTo != entry.url %}<span class="badge badge-redirect" title="Measured through a redirect to {{ entry.redirectTo }}{% if entry.redirectConfirmed %} — confirmed, so history will follow it{% else %} — {{ entry.redirectReason }}{% endif %}">&#8618; redirect</span>
					{% elif entry.canonicalUrl %}<span class="badge badge-redirect" title="Measured through a redirect to {{ entry.canonicalUrl }} — the site's canonical form of this URL">&#8618; redirect</span>{% endif %}
					{% if entry.unlisted %}<span class="badge badge-unlisted" title="Built with {{ entry.unlisted.generator }} but not in {{ entry.unlisted.groupName }} — nobody has submitted it to the 11ty/11ty-community repository">unlisted</span>{% endif %}
				</td>

				{% if entry.latest %}
					{# 22 rather than the shortcode's 16: these are the row's only
					   pictures, in a column of their own, and at 16 they read as
					   punctuation beside the numbers. The inline uses elsewhere — a
					   tally row, a stat, a heading — sit next to text and keep the
					   default. #}
					<td class="col-stack">
						{% if entry.generator %}{% stackIcon entry.generator, 22 %}
						{% elif entry.presumedGenerator %}{% stackIcon entry.presumedGenerator, 22 %}
						{% else %}<span class="stack-none" title="No generator detected">·</span>{% endif %}
					</td>
					<td class="col-stack">
						{% if entry.host %}{% stackIcon entry.host, 22 %}{% else %}<span class="stack-none" title="No host detected">·</span>{% endif %}
					</td>
					{#
						Six rings, in the order <speedlify2-score> draws them: the four
						Lighthouse categories, then Core Web Vitals and axe — the ranking's
						two tiebreakers in the order it applies them, and the two things
						Lighthouse's own categories do not cover. One column rather than
						three, so a row reads as one picture and matches the embeddable
						badge exactly.

						No total on the end, unlike the category tables: every row in this
						one is on {{ perfectTotal }} by definition, so it would be the same
						number all the way down.
					#}
					<td class="col-rings">
						<span class="rings">
						{% for score in metrics.SCORES %}
							{% scoreRing entry.latest.lab.scores[score.key], score.label %}
						{% endfor %}
						{% axeRing entry.axe %}
						{% cwvRing entry.cwvFailures, entry.cwv.assessed, entry.cwvWorst %}
						</span>
					</td>
					<td class="col-trend">{% sparkline entry.trends.performance %}</td>
					<td class="num col-lcp"><span class="lcp-cell"><span class="lcp-value">{{ entry.latest.lab.timings.lcp | ms }}</span>{% lcpBar entry.latest.lab.lcpBreakdown, entry.latest.lab.timings.lcp %}</span></td>
					<td class="num">{{ entry.latest.lab.timings.cls | unit("") }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ entry.latest.lab.timings.tbt | ms }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ entry.latest.lab.weight.total | bytes }}</td>
					<td class="num">{{ entry.latest.lab.weight.requests | num }}</td>
					<td class="num">{% if entry.labVsField %}<span class="lvf" title="Lab LCP {{ entry.labVsField.lab | ms }}, field p75 {{ entry.labVsField.field | ms }}">{{ entry.labVsField.ratio }}&times;</span>{% else %}<span class="muted">&mdash;</span>{% endif %}</td>
					<td class="num col-nojs">{% if entry.ownScreenshot and entry.ownScreenshot.difference !== null %}{{ entry.ownScreenshot.difference }}%{% if entry.clientRendered %}<span class="csr-yes" title="{{ entry.ownScreenshot.noJsEmpty }}% of the no-JS capture is empty rows, so the page drew nothing without its scripts"><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation csr-icon" width="12" height="12" aria-hidden="true"></i>EMPTY</span>{% endif %}{% else %}<span class="muted">&mdash;</span>{% endif %}</td>
					<td class="col-cats">
						{#- Names and ids are index-aligned, so the id for a name is its
						    position in the other list. #}
						<span class="cat-chips">{% for name in entry.groupNames %}<a class="cat-chip" href="/group/{{ entry.groups[loop.index0] }}/">{{ name }}</a>{% endfor %}</span>
					</td>
				{% else %}
					<td class="num muted" colspan="12">
						{% if entry.currentlyFailing %}every attempt failed{% else %}awaiting first measurement{% endif %}
					</td>
				{% endif %}
			</tr>
		{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>
</section>

{% if sites.stacks.generators.items.length or sites.stacks.hosts.items.length %}
{{ technology(sites.stacks, "stacks") }}
{% endif %}

{% if sites.moving.length %}
<section class="redirects" id="moving">
	<h2>{{ sites.moving.length | plural("Site") }} that {{ sites.moving.length | verb("redirects", "redirect") }}</h2>
	<p class="muted">
		Lighthouse follows a redirect, so these are measured at their
		destination. A redirect move may be permanent enough to maintain a link
		to the <em>history</em> of the previous destination, which happens on
		its own once a redirect proves stable (when multiple consecutive equal 
		redirects have been measured).
	</p>

	{#
		Grouped by verdict rather than listed flat. A hundred rows each stamped
		with the same machine-readable reason tells you nothing; the reason is
		the interesting part, so it becomes the heading and is said once.
	#}
	{% set reasons = [] %}
	{% for move in sites.moving %}
		{% set key = "confirmed" if move.confirmed else (move.reason or "unknown") %}
		{% if key not in reasons %}{% set reasons = (reasons.push(key), reasons) %}{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}

	{% for reason in reasons %}
	{% set group = [] %}
	{% for move in sites.moving %}
		{% if ("confirmed" if move.confirmed else (move.reason or "unknown")) == reason %}
			{% set group = (group.push(move), group) %}
		{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}

	{#
		"Waiting for more measurements" is the largest group and the least
		interesting: nothing has happened to these yet, and the confirmation
		process needs no attention to run. Collapsed so the groups that do mean
		something — an unstable destination, a temporary redirect — are not buried
		under a table of pending ones.
	#}
	{% set collapsed = reason == "not-enough-measurements" %}
	{% if collapsed %}
	<details class="redirects-pending">
		{#
			The flex row is a span inside the summary, not the summary itself: a
			summary whose own display is changed stops being the disclosure widget,
			and the details renders permanently open.
		#}
		<summary><span class="redirects-head">{{ reason | redirectReason }}<span class="redirects-count">{{ group.length | num }}</span></span></summary>
	{% else %}
	<h3 class="redirects-head">
		{% if reason == "confirmed" %}Confirmed — history follows{% else %}{{ reason | redirectReason }}{% endif %}
		<span class="redirects-count">{{ group.length | num }}</span>
	</h3>
	{% endif %}
	<p class="muted small">
		{% if reason == "confirmed" %}
		The destination has been consistent and permanent across several runs, so
		measurement has already followed it and the history came along.
		{% else %}{{ reason | redirectExplanation }}{% endif %}
	</p>
	{#
		The same scrolling grid the leaderboards use. Two full URLs per row is
		more than the column can hold on a narrow screen, and a table that
		scrolls inside its own box beats one that wraps every row to three lines
		or pushes the page sideways.
	#}
	<div class="table-scroll">
	<table class="grid compact">
		<thead>
			<tr>
				<th class="col-url">Redirect</th>
				<th class="col-url">Categories</th>
			</tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			{% for move in group %}
			<tr>
				{#
					Both URLs in one cell. Split across two columns the arrow sat at the
					start of the second one, and the gap between columns grew to the
					width of the longest source URL on the page — so the pair read as
					two unrelated lists rather than as one redirect.
				#}
				<td class="col-url">
					<a href="/site/{{ move.slug }}/">{{ move.from }}</a>
					<span class="redirects-arrow">&rarr;</span>
					<code>{{ move.to }}</code>
				</td>
				{# `cat`, not `group` — the outer loop is already using that name. #}
				<td class="col-url">{% for cat in move.groups %}<span class="cat-chip">{{ cat }}</span>{% endfor %}</td>
			</tr>
			{% endfor %}
		</tbody>
	</table>
	</div>
	{% if collapsed %}</details>{% endif %}
	{% endfor %}
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if sites.orphans.length %}
<section class="orphans" id="orphans">
	<h2>Orphaned {{ sites.orphans.length | plural("history","histories") }}</h2>
	<p class="muted">
		These URLs have stored measurements but are no longer in
		<code>config/sites.js</code>. Their history is kept, not deleted.
	</p>
	<ul>
		{% for orphan in sites.orphans %}
		<li><code>{{ orphan.url or orphan.hash }}</code>{% if orphan.name %} — {{ orphan.name }}{% endif %}</li>
		{% endfor %}
	</ul>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if sites.archived.length %}
{#
	Last on the page, and deliberately inert. Archiving is for a URL that should
	stop being pointed at — a site that has gone, or one that should not be sent
	traffic — so there is no row, no rank and no link, here or anywhere: an
	archived site has no page of its own either, since those are generated from
	the entries it was removed from.

	Still named, because the alternative is a silent disappearance. The record
	stays on disk; this is what says so.
#}
<section class="orphans" id="archived">
	<h2>Archived</h2>
	<p class="muted">
		{{ sites.archived.length | countOf("site") }} kept are on record but taken
		out of circulation: no longer ranked or linked. These sites have lapsed or
		their domain may be parked by a reseller.
	</p>
	<p class="muted small">
		A site that fails {{ sites.config.archiveAfterFailures }} measurements in a
		row is archived automatically. It may return to the board if it comes back
		to the land of the living.
	</p>
	{#
		Counted, not named. Archiving is for sites that should stop being pointed
		at — a lapsed domain, a parked one — and listing them here would keep doing
		exactly that, in plain text instead of a link. The count says the record
		exists; config/archived.json says which.
	#}
</section>
{% endif %}
